\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8.2.1}{\ignorespaces AVL's whole job in one picture. Inserting $1,2,3$ (a mini chain $C$) pushes node $1$'s balance factor to $-2$: its two subtrees now differ in height by two, violating $|\bal  |\le 1$. That is the RR case, cured by a single \texttt  {rotateLeft(1)} that lifts $2$ to the root: $2\to (1,3)$, every balance factor legal, height back to $1$, inorder untouched. The same repair, run on the full $1,\dots  ,7$, yields the balanced twin of Figure~\ref {fig:c06-chain}.}}{489}{figure.caption.86}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:c06-avlfig}{{8.2.1}{489}{AVL's whole job in one picture. Inserting $1,2,3$ (a mini chain $C$) pushes node $1$'s balance factor to $-2$: its two subtrees now differ in height by two, violating $|\bal |\le 1$. That is the RR case, cured by a single \texttt {rotateLeft(1)} that lifts $2$ to the root: $2\to (1,3)$, every balance factor legal, height back to $1$, inorder untouched. The same repair, run on the full $1,\dots ,7$, yields the balanced twin of \figref {c06-chain}}{figure.caption.86}{}}
