\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {8.5.1}Intuition}{516}{subsection.8.5.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{def:c06-splay}{{8.5.1}{516}{Splaying a node to the root}{tcb@cnt@definition.8.5.1}{}}
\newlabel{def:c06-zigzig}{{8.5.2}{516}{Zig, zig-zig and zig-zag}{tcb@cnt@definition.8.5.2}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8.5.1}{\ignorespaces The two two-level splay moves (the ZIG is just the single closing rotation). In \textbf  {zig-zig} the three nodes were collinear; rotating first about the grandparent, then the parent, lifts $x$ two levels \emph  {and} promotes $p$, leaving $x\to p\to g$ as a short left spine -- the whole local path flattened. In \textbf  {zig-zag} $x$ sat in the middle; one rotation each way about $p$ then $g$ brings the bent $x$ to the top of the triple. The ``rotate the grandparent first'' order in zig-zig is precisely what makes the amortised bound work.}}{516}{figure.caption.89}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:c06-splayfig}{{8.5.1}{516}{The two two-level splay moves (the ZIG is just the single closing rotation). In \textbf {zig-zig} the three nodes were collinear; rotating first about the grandparent, then the parent, lifts $x$ two levels \emph {and} promotes $p$, leaving $x\to p\to g$ as a short left spine -- the whole local path flattened. In \textbf {zig-zag} $x$ sat in the middle; one rotation each way about $p$ then $g$ brings the bent $x$ to the top of the triple. The ``rotate the grandparent first'' order in zig-zig is precisely what makes the amortised bound work}{figure.caption.89}{}}
